    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2B6uiUNKLQGdsXe278xZbYD)_\n\nThe tag chip colours in `sp-select-note` / `sp-insert-note` no longer use Notion's fixed hex pairs; they follow the active theme.\n\n- `sp-org-tag-groups-palette` keeps its ten slot names (`default`, `gray`, … `red`), so `sp-org-tag-groups-colors` is unchanged. Each slot now names a face (`error`, `success`, `link`, `link-visited`, `font-lock-keyword-face`, …), a colour, or a mix `(A B WEIGHT)`; `nil` is neutral. The sp themes colour those faces from their palette, so any theme works through them.\n- New `sp-org-tag-groups-palette-entry`: hue blended into the `default` background (0.22 light, 0.32 dark, as in #246), foreground nudged until contrast is at least 4.5.\n- Recomputed on theme change via `sp-after-load-theme-hook`.\n- A group with no colour, such as `unsorted` from #248, gets the neutral `default`. I did not touch the group list or colours, to avoid conflicts with #248.\n- Doc (`.org`) and tangled `.el` edited together; CHANGELOG entry added; new `tests/sp-org-tag-groups-test.el` (5 tests, pass in Emacs 29 batch).\n\nNot checked in a GUI: how the chips look in each theme. The face-to-slot mapping (e.g. `yellow` = `warning`, `pink` = `link-visited`) is my guess; adjust the palette if a colour looks off.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNi9a5fkhXpCewRCyWNk)_",
